Track 1: Cold Hemp Stitches the Estuary
- Story & Context: It began when the bulk freighter Cisne Negro drifted blind into the Calumet River basin, blaring its 40-hertz Tyfon horn into the greasy mist only to be answered three seconds later by the inbound Maersk Vespucio sounding a minor third below. On the freezing concrete pier, dockworker Baltazar Cruz slammed his rusted iron shaker against a fuel drum to synchronize with the two-tone maritime barrage, his portable garage-house rig feeding the horn feedback into a runaway tape loop that made the crane operators drop their levers in terror. The crowd in drenched oilskins convulsed as the two giants bellowed through the soot, screaming along with the distorted PA hook: "Pull the iron seam tight before the horn drowns us all." By dawn, the shipmasters were arrested for navigational negligence, but the groove had already infected every freight dock from Indiana Harbor to Veracruz.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro close-up of thick, fraying maritime hemp ropes bound around a barnacle-encrusted iron cleat, with glowing bioluminescent brine seeping into the fibers under a heavy mist.

Track 2: "Did You Unpick the Hawser, Mateo?"
- Story & Context: Huddled beneath the gantry cranes at Slip 4 with a battered four-track cassette deck, Mateo Morales confessed to the night shift that he had deliberately severed the steel tow-lines of the grain barges so their adrift hulls would continue groaning against the timber pilings in syncopated swing. The bitter, copper tang of his own bitten tongue mingled with grease smoke as he watched harbor patrol boats circle in the whiteout fog, blindly responding to his sub-bass organ pedal with deafening air-horn blasts that vibrated the marrow in his teeth. He clutched his lead-shot gourd shaker until his knuckles cracked white, weeping openly into his microphone while loop-layering the freighters' panic calls: "I let the hawser fray just to hear your belly mourn." His confession became an outlaw garage anthem across the loading docks, proving that love in the estuaries was just another vessel lost without radar.
