Track 3: Azimuth Bias Over the Sunken Gantry
- Story & Context: Decades after the dry docks were abandoned to black brackish water, harbor pilots at Basin Twelve still swear the mud itself preserves the low-end reverberations of those gargantuan ships hailing each other across the dead channels. Local divers salvage old shipping containers draped in cold, slimy eelgrass, finding that the trapped air pockets inside still ring like cavernous cathedral halls when struck with a wrench. The myth persists that Sister Inés walked into the blinding white tide with an electric accordion strapped to her ribs, permanently wedding the cumbia scraper to the phantom 120-BPM drone of fog-bound diesel freighters that never reached port. Now, lone deckhands leaning over salt-caked railings late at night still hear her gospel drift up through the churning propeller froth: "No chart will save the hull that dances with the fog." The aftermath is an empty harbor that never sleeps, eternally swaying to the ghost calls of steel giants that refuse to sink.
